   "golden": "A fungus is not a plant. It has a cell wall, so it looks plant-like on paper — but that wall is chitin, not cellulose, there are no chloroplasts, and it never photosynthesises: it digests its food outside its body and absorbs it. And a mushroom is only the fruiting part; the fungus itself is the mycelium of hyphae in the log or the soil.",
   "examFocus": [
    {
     "tag": "State",
     "text": "Animal: many cells, nucleus, no cell wall, no chloroplasts, heterotrophic nutrition. Plant: many cells, nucleus, cellulose cell wall, chloroplasts, autotrophic nutrition by photosynthesis. Two differences are usually enough for the marks."
    },
    {
     "tag": "Supplement",
     "text": "Fungus: cell wall of chitin, not cellulose; no chloroplasts; cells joined into hyphae; saprotrophic nutrition (heterotrophic). Prokaryote: no nucleus, circular DNA, plasmids, no mitochondria. Protoctist: single cells with a nucleus; some like plants, some like animals."
    },
    {
     "tag": "Classify",
     "text": "Given a description — \"a single cell with a nucleus and chloroplasts\" — name the kingdom (protoctist) and the feature that decided it."
    }
   ],
